<GuideLayout
  title="INA219 Battery Charger Monitor - Never Charge Blind"
  description="Build a real-time battery charger monitor with an INA219 current sensor, a TP4056 charger module, and a GC9A01 round display. Watch the full CC/CV charge curve unfold and spot misbehaving chargers before they cook a cell."
  toc={[
    {href: '#what-youll-learn', label: 'What You Will Learn'},
    {href: '#hardware', label: 'Hardware'},
    {href: '#wiring', label: 'Wiring'},
    {href: '#sketch', label: 'The Sketch'},
    {href: '#what-youll-see', label: 'What You Will See'},
    {href: '#misbehaviour', label: 'What Misbehaviour Looks Like'},
    {href: '#going-further', label: 'Going Further'},
    {href: '#safety', label: 'Safety'},
  ]}
>

  <div class="hero">
    <div class="category">Power &bull; ESP32</div>
    <h1>INA219 Battery Charger Monitor<br>Never Charge Blind</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Board:</strong> ESP32 Dev Board</div>
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>Topic:</strong> INA219, current sensing, GC9A01 display, battery charging</div>
    </div>
    <div class="badge badge-untested">&#9888; Untested</div>
  </div>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <div class="section" id="intro">
    <p>If you are charging lithium cells, you should be watching what the charger is actually doing. Bad modules cook batteries. Counterfeit protection ICs lie. Salvaged cells from old laptop packs accept current in ways that depend on their history, and you cannot tell a tired cell from a healthy one without measuring. None of this is paranoia. It is just the cost of working with chemistry that wants to release its energy all at once if something goes wrong.</p>
    <p>This guide builds the simplest possible answer to "what is actually happening on this charger." An INA219 sits inline between a TP4056 charger module and a single 18650 cell. An ESP32 reads voltage and current from the INA219 and renders both on a round GC9A01 display. The meter sits next to the charger, watching, all the way from a flat cell to a full one. You see the entire charge curve unfold on the screen.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- What You Will Learn -->
  <div class="section" id="what-youll-learn">
    <h2>What You Will Learn</h2>
    <p>The INA219 is a high-side current sensor with an I2C interface. It measures the voltage drop across a small shunt resistor in the power line and converts that to current using Ohm's law internally. It also measures the bus voltage on the load side. Two readings, one chip, three wires plus power and ground.</p>
    <p>You will learn the wiring, the I2C registers (or rather, the library that hides them), and how to render the result on a circular display in a way that reads at a glance.</p>
    <p>You will also learn what a healthy charge cycle looks like. The current starts high and stays roughly constant. The voltage climbs slowly. Then the voltage hits 4.2V, the current starts tapering, and the cell sips the last few percent until the charger calls it done. That shape is called CC/CV - constant current, then constant voltage. It is the canonical safe lithium charge profile, and once you have watched it happen on your own bench you will recognise the shape forever.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Hardware -->
  <div class="section" id="hardware">
    <h2>Hardware</h2>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>ESP32 Dev Board - the classic 30-pin or 38-pin module on a breadboard-friendly carrier</li>
      <li>INA219 breakout - common modules from any maker supplier all use the same chip and pinout. Default I2C address is 0x40</li>
      <li>GC9A01 240x240 round display - SPI interface, four data wires plus power</li>
      <li>TP4056 module with battery protection - the two-IC version. The bare TP4056 works but a protected module is safer for bench experiments</li>
      <li>Single 18650 cell - any healthy cell. If you are charging a salvaged or unknown cell, that is exactly what this rig is for</li>
      <li>18650 holder, or clip leads for brief testing</li>
      <li>Separate USB power sources for the TP4056 and the ESP32 - the ESP32 cannot share power with the cell being measured, otherwise the INA219 sees the chip's current overhead too</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Wiring -->
  <div class="section" id="wiring">
    <h2>Wiring</h2>
    <p>The INA219 sits between the charger and the battery, in series on the positive line. Negative is shared throughout.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`USB power in
     │
     ▼
┌──────────┐
│ TP4056   │
│   B+ ────┼────► VIN+ on INA219
│   B- ────┼─────────────────────┐
└──────────┘                     │
                  VIN- on INA219 ┼────► Battery +
                                 │
                                 ▼
                              Battery -
                                 │
                                 ▼
                            Common ground`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p>The INA219 is powered from the ESP32's 3.3V rail and connects via I2C. The GC9A01 is on SPI. Full pin table for an ESP32 Dev Board:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>ESP32 pin</th><th>Connects to</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>GPIO 21 (SDA)</td><td>INA219 SDA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 22 (SCL)</td><td>INA219 SCL</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3.3V</td><td>INA219 VCC, GC9A01 VCC</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GND</td><td>INA219 GND, GC9A01 GND, battery negative</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 18</td><td>GC9A01 SCK</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 23</td><td>GC9A01 MOSI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 5</td><td>GC9A01 CS</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 27</td><td>GC9A01 DC</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 4</td><td>GC9A01 RST</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPIO 15</td><td>GC9A01 BL (backlight)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>These are the standard ESP32 default I2C and VSPI pins. If you are using a different board variant, check its pinout and adjust accordingly. The TP4056 and ESP32 are on separate USB supplies.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- The Sketch -->
  <div class="section" id="sketch">
    <h2>The Sketch</h2>
    <p>Uses the Adafruit INA219 library and Arduino_GFX_Library by Moon On Our Nation. Both install from the Arduino IDE library manager. The display redraws at 5 frames per second - fast enough for charging dynamics, slow enough to not overwhelm the SPI bus.</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>{`/*
 * We stand on the shoulders of giants when we build
 * with knowledge gained from others' efforts.
 * That doesn't make us giants. Be humble.
 * Create with care. Open source is the way.
 *
 * INA219 Battery Charger Monitor
 * --------------------------------
 * Reads voltage and current from a TP4056 charging an 18650 cell.
 * Renders two arc gauges on a GC9A01 240x240 round display.
 * Wire INA219 inline between TP4056 B+ and battery positive.
 *
 * Board:   ESP32 Dev Board
 * Library: Adafruit INA219 by Adafruit
 *          Arduino_GFX_Library by Moon On Our Nation
 *
 * Wiring (ESP32 Dev Board):
 *   INA219: SDA -> GPIO21   SCL -> GPIO22   VCC -> 3.3V   GND -> GND
 *   GC9A01: SCK -> GPIO18   MOSI -> GPIO23  CS  -> GPIO5
 *           DC  -> GPIO27   RST  -> GPIO4   BL  -> GPIO15
 *
 * Open source - MIT Licence
 * Electronic Zoology - field notes from the garage
 * https://electroniczoology.com/guides/esp32-ina219-charger-monitor
 */

#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_INA219.h>
#include <Arduino_GFX_Library.h>

#define TFT_DC  27
#define TFT_CS   5
#define TFT_RST  4
#define TFT_SCK  18
#define TFT_MOSI 23
#define TFT_BL   15

Arduino_DataBus *bus = new Arduino_HWSPI(TFT_DC, TFT_CS, TFT_SCK, TFT_MOSI);
Arduino_GFX *gfx = new Arduino_GC9A01(bus, TFT_RST, 0, true);

Adafruit_INA219 ina219;

const int CX = 120;
const int CY = 120;

const uint32_t SAMPLE_MS = 200;
uint32_t lastSample = 0;

void drawArc(int cx, int cy, int radius, int thickness,
             float startAngle, float endAngle, uint16_t colour) {
  for (float a = startAngle; a <= endAngle; a += 0.5) {
    float rad = a * 3.14159 / 180.0;
    for (int t = 0; t < thickness; t++) {
      int x = cx + (radius - t) * cos(rad);
      int y = cy + (radius - t) * sin(rad);
      gfx->drawPixel(x, y, colour);
    }
  }
}

void drawCentered(const String &s, int y, uint16_t color) {
  int16_t x1, y1;
  uint16_t w, h;
  gfx->setTextColor(color, 0x0000);
  gfx->getTextBounds(s.c_str(), 0, 0, &x1, &y1, &w, &h);
  gfx->setCursor(CX - w / 2, y);
  gfx->print(s);
}

void drawMeters(float volts, float amps) {
  gfx->fillScreen(0x0000);

  // Voltage arc - top half, scaled 3.0 to 4.2V
  float vFraction = constrain((volts - 3.0) / 1.2, 0.0, 1.0);
  drawArc(CX, CY, 110, 8, 180, 180 + 180 * vFraction, 0x07FF); // cyan

  // Current arc - bottom half, scaled 0 to 1000mA
  float aFraction = constrain(amps / 1000.0, 0.0, 1.0);
  drawArc(CX, CY, 90, 8, 0, 180 * aFraction, 0x07E0); // green

  gfx->setTextSize(3);
  drawCentered(String(volts, 2) + "V", CY - 20, 0x07FF);
  drawCentered(String((int)amps) + "mA", CY + 20, 0x07E0);
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);

  Wire.begin(21, 22);

  if (!ina219.begin()) {
    Serial.println("INA219 not found - check wiring");
    while (1) delay(1000);
  }

  pinMode(TFT_BL, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(TFT_BL, HIGH);

  gfx->begin();
  gfx->fillScreen(0x0000);

  Serial.println("Charger monitor running");
}

void loop() {
  if (millis() - lastSample >= SAMPLE_MS) {
    lastSample = millis();

    float busVoltage = ina219.getBusVoltage_V();
    float current_mA = ina219.getCurrent_mA();

    Serial.printf("V: %.3f  I: %.1f mA\\n", busVoltage, current_mA);

    drawMeters(busVoltage, current_mA);
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <p>If you see flicker on the display, switch to sprite-based rendering using Arduino_GFX's <code>Arduino_Canvas</code> class to double-buffer the frame before pushing it to the screen.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- What You Will See -->
  <div class="section" id="what-youll-see">
    <h2>What You Will See</h2>
    <p>Plug a flat 18650 into the holder. The TP4056 starts charging. Within seconds the display lights up with two arcs and two numbers.</p>
    <p>A flat cell, freshly connected, reads something like 3.05V and the charger pours in close to 1A. Both arcs are dramatic - the voltage arc starts barely visible, the current arc is nearly full. Watch for a few minutes and the voltage arc creeps clockwise as the cell takes charge. The current arc holds steady. This is the constant current phase.</p>
    <p>After 30 to 60 minutes of solid current, the voltage arc reaches 4.2V at the top of its range. At this moment the charger transitions to constant voltage. The current arc starts shrinking. Voltage stays nearly pinned at 4.2V. The current tapers from 800mA down to 400mA, then 200mA, then 100mA, then below 50mA over maybe another half hour. When the current drops below the TP4056's threshold - typically 1/10th of the set charge current - the charger calls it done and the LED switches from red to blue. Current goes to ~0mA. Voltage settles at 4.2V.</p>
    <p>That entire sequence is the lithium charge profile in plain sight. Memorise the shape. Once you have seen it, you can spot a misbehaving charger immediately because it does not look like that.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Misbehaviour -->
  <div class="section" id="misbehaviour">
    <h2>What Misbehaviour Looks Like</h2>
    <p>A few common failure modes you can now spot:</p>
    <ul class="flag-list">
      <li><strong>Overcharging.</strong> A counterfeit TP4056 that does not drop into CV will keep pumping near-full current until the cell hits 4.3V or higher. The voltage arc shoots past 4.2V instead of plateauing. Disconnect immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>Wrong charge current.</strong> A mismatched set resistor might charge at 1500mA when you expected 500mA. The current arc is fuller than expected from the start. Some cells handle this, some do not. Knowing the actual current lets you check it against the cell's rated charge rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Tired or damaged cell.</strong> A high-impedance cell takes very little current even when empty. The current arc never gets above 200mA. Not worth using for anything that demands real capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>Short or near-short.</strong> Current pegs at maximum, voltage arc stays near zero, and the TP4056 either trips its protection or starts getting hot. Disconnect.</li>
      <li><strong>Dead cell.</strong> Charge it, watch the arc settle to 4.2V, then unplug the USB and watch the voltage arc immediately drop to 3.9V or lower. That is a cell that holds no real charge.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Without a meter, all of these failures look identical: a charger module with a red LED. With this rig, each one has a distinct visual signature.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Going Further -->
  <div class="section" id="going-further">
    <h2>Going Further</h2>
    <ul class="flag-list">
      <li><strong>Two cells at once.</strong> Add a second INA219 on I2C address 0x41 (jumper A0 high on the breakout) and monitor two cells simultaneously. Useful for matched-pair testing before building a pack.</li>
      <li><strong>Log the charge curve.</strong> Write readings to LittleFS and graph the shape afterward. A healthy cell and a tired cell produce visibly different curves over the full cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Remote display.</strong> Send readings over ESP-NOW to a second ESP32 so the meter sits next to the charger while the display is across the room.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Safety -->
  <div class="section" id="safety">
    <h2>Safety</h2>
    <p>Lithium cells are fine when treated well and dangerous when not. Do not leave any charging rig unattended on a flammable surface. Keep a metal or ceramic dish under the cell holder while you are characterising an unknown charger. Disconnect immediately if anything heats up unexpectedly.</p>
    <p>The INA219 is rated to 26V on the bus voltage. A single lithium cell at 4.2V is well within range. If you adapt this rig for multi-cell packs, verify that your bus voltage stays under 26V.</p>
    <p>The whole point of this rig is to give you the information you need to know when something is wrong - but you still have to look at it.</p>
  </div>

